{% if mode == "generate" -%}
You are building a fair, transparent scoring rubric for one job posting.

TITLE: {{ title }}

ENTIRE STRUCTURED JD PAYLOAD (valid JSON — read the skills, responsibilities,
schedule/shift policies, work locations, visa, and everything else from this):

{{ jd_json }}

Respond with ONLY a single JSON object — no text before or after — with EXACTLY
this shape:

{
  "position_title": "<the role title>",
  "categories": [
    {
      "key": "snake_case_key_1",
      "label": "Human Label 1",
      "max": 20,
      "icon": "•",
      "anchors": [
        {"min_points": 0, "band": "no credible evidence of this yet"},
        {"min_points": 10, "band": "some evidence, gaps remain"},
        {"min_points": 20, "band": "clear evidence matching the JD wording"}
      ],
      "jd_sources": ["<a literal string copied verbatim from the JD payload>"],
      "requirement_text": "<the exact required-skill or responsibility wording this category scores>"
    }
  ],
  "bonus_max": 10,
  "bonus_signals": ["exceptional, role-relevant signals that deserve bonus points"],
  "derivation": {
    "scoreable": ["<every required skill and responsibility that counts toward the score>"],
    "eligibility": ["<schedule/shift/location/sponsorship requirements - NOT scored, with their exact JD wording>"],
    "removed": [
      {"field": "<noise that is not in the JD at all>", "role": "noise", "reason": "<why it was dropped>"}
    ]
  }
}

Score-level anchors and the partition trail are the points of the whole rubric,
so the rules matter:

STEP 1 - Partition every signal you can extract from the JD:
- Requirements with a floor (skills, years of experience, degree, certifications)
  -> category.jd_sources / requirement_text and derivation.scoreable. NEVER
  leave a stated required skill or responsibility out of the scoreable set.
- Schedule / shift / work-location / office-policy requirements (for example
  "camera on", "on-site", "work in Kandivali", "24x7 shifts", "hybrid") are
  ELIGIBILITY, not scoreable. Do exactly that even when they appear under
  "responsibilities". They are never scored and never penalize category scores.
- Genuine noise that the JD does not state at all -> derivation.removed with a
  reason. Do NOT add removed items that echo a required skill or responsibility.

STEP 2 - Ground everything in the literal JD wording:
- jd_sources entries MUST be copied verbatim from the payload above (no
  paraphrasing, no invention). derivation.scoreable must echo every required
  skill and responsibility verbatim.
- Anchors: 2-5 bands per category, strictly ascending min_points starting at 0,
  top band at most the category max. Anchors describe resume evidence, never
  custom vocabulary.
- Do NOT use client/customer/stakeholder or production/ops/incident/uptime
  language in any band unless the payload above actually contains those words.

Guidelines:
- 3-5 categories; weights should not all be identical; sum of all max values
  should land between 10 and 100. bonus_max must be an integer between 0 and 20.
- Category weights must never depend on a candidate's name, gender, institution,
  or location.
{%- else -%}
You are evaluating a resume for the {{ position_title }}. Analyze the resume data
and provide scores based on these criteria:

**MANDATORY: You MUST always fill ALL categories: {{ category_keys }}.**

## SCORING CRITERIA (pick each score using the JD-anchored bands)
{% if default_bands -%}
## SHARED SCORE BANDS (apply to every category that does not list its own below)
{% for anchor in default_bands -%}
- {{ anchor.min_points }} pts: {{ anchor.band }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% for cat in categories -%}
### {{ cat.label }} (0-{{ cat.max }} points)
{% if cat.requirement_text -%}
- JD requirement this category scores: {{ cat.requirement_text }}
{% endif %}
{% if cat.custom_bands -%}
{% for anchor in cat.anchors or [] -%}
- {{ anchor.min_points }} pts: {{ anchor.band }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
## BONUS POINTS (Maximum total: {{ bonus_max }} points)
{% if bonus_signals -%}
- Award bonus points only for: {{ bonus_signals | join("; ") }}
{% else -%}
- Award bonus points for exceptional, role-relevant signals beyond the criteria.
{% endif %}

## ELIGIBILITY (hard requirements, reported separately from the score)
- Set eligibility.eligible = true unless a hard/flat requirement (schedule,
  shift, location, office policy, sponsorship, degree) is clearly unmet.
- List each unmet hard requirement in eligibility.blocked_reasons AND echo it in
  critical_gaps. An unmet hard requirement never lowers category scores.

## RESPONSE RULES
1. You MUST respond with ONLY the JSON structure below; no text before or after.
2. Fill ALL score categories; each score 0..max using the bands above.
3. Provide quoted, resume-specific evidence per category and an
   evidence_strength 0-3 (3 = evidence directly matches the JD wording).
4. critical_gaps = genuinely missing required skills/conditions named in the JD.

{
    "scores": {
{% for cat in categories -%}
        "{{ cat.key }}": {"score": 0, "max": {{ cat.max }}, "evidence": "string", "evidence_strength": 2}}{{ "," if not loop.last }}
{% endfor %}
    },
    "bonus_points": {"total": 0, "breakdown": "string"},
    "eligibility": {"eligible": true, "blocked_reasons": []},
    "critical_gaps": ["genuinely missing requirement"],
    "key_strengths": ["strength1", "strength2"],
    "areas_for_improvement": ["improvement1"]
}

## CALIBRATION EXAMPLE (illustrative — never copy its text as this resume's evidence)
This is a WORKED EXAMPLE of band usage for a generic role. It is NOT the resume
you are scoring. Use it only to calibrate how strictly to apply 0..max per band.

Example role: "Backend Engineer (illustrative)". A candidate with a real shipped
API, no mentoring duties and no open source scored:
- api_design (max 10): 8 — band "solid shipped API evidence"
- collaboration (max 10): 3 — band "some evidence, clear gaps remain"
- mentorship (max 10): 0 — band "no evidence at all"
Use the full 0..max range; a category max is rarely the score for partial evidence.

Resume to evaluate:

